  "response": "When two languages share a striking grammatical feature, adjudicating between common descent and contact-induced transfer begins with the instruments used to establish relatedness in the first place. The comparative method establishes genetic relationship through regular, recurrent sound correspondences, while calquing transfers a grammatical pattern from one language to another without transferring phonological material; a diagnosis anchored in recurrent sound matches and a mode of transfer that carries no phonological material whatever cannot both be true. Each mechanism leaves a different residue in the material of the languages, and it is that residue, rather than the salience of the resemblance itself, that the analyst must weigh.\n\nThe alternatives can be put sharply. At least one of two histories must be true, and perhaps both: the shared feature was inherited by both languages from their common ancestor, or the shared feature passed from one speech community to the other through bilingual contact. That a grammatical pattern can be transplanted with none of its phonological substance, as the process described above demonstrates, is evidence for the second route, the movement of the feature across a bilingual boundary. Shared arbitrary morphological irregularities are strong evidence of descent from a common ancestor; in other words, idiosyncratic paradigm anomalies held in common by two languages point to inheritance from a shared parent language. Where such anomalies can be exhibited, they show that the feature came down to both languages from the parent rather than travelling between them.\n\nWeighing the two histories depends further on known asymmetries in what diffuses across language boundaries. Basic vocabulary resists borrowing more strongly than cultural vocabulary, and bound inflectional morphology is transferred between languages far less often than free lexical items; a hierarchy that makes core lexemes the most resistant material of all cannot be squared with one that makes bound inflection rarer in transfer than any free word. Grammatical morphology is never transferred between languages in situations of contact; that categorical exclusion cannot be squared with the gradient finding that bound inflection merely transfers less readily than free lexical material.\n\nWhat ultimately settles a dispute of this kind is chronology, both internal and external. Reconstruction showing that a feature was absent in the proto-language indicates that the feature is a later innovation. Philological work has an object of its own: dated written records fix the earliest attestation of a grammatical feature in a language's history. Where the reconstructed history and the documentary record converge on a date later than the separation of the two languages, the ancestral account falls away and transfer remains the only available history.\n\nAlbanian, Romanian, and Bulgarian all place the definite article after the noun, and that common postposition shows the Balkan Sprachbund to comprise Albanian, Romanian, Greek, Bulgarian, and Macedonian. The shared features of the Balkan languages are inherited directly from Proto-Indo-European; yet that genealogical account cannot be squared with the composition of the convergence area just described, whose members are drawn from branches far too distantly related for such traits to have survived in common. Adjudication, then, rests not on the striking resemblance itself but on the joint testimony of reconstruction, dated attestation, and areal distribution.",
